Chapter 2

Mathematical Preliminaries and the Stochastic Ceiling

2.1 Basic Definitions

We assume the reader is familiar with fundamental concepts in measure‑theoretic probability, optimization, formal logic, and differential geometry. This section fixes notation and recalls the core objects that will be used throughout.

Definition 2.1 (Probability Space). A probability space is a triple (\Omega, \mathcal{F}, P), where \Omega is a sample space, \mathcal{F} is a \sigma-algebra, and P is a probability measure.

Definition 2.2 (Kullback–Leibler Divergence). For two probability distributions p and q on (\Omega, \mathcal{F}) with p \ll q, the KL divergence is

D_{\text{KL}}(p \| q) = \int_\Omega \log\left(\frac{dp}{dq}\right) dp .

Definition 2.3 (Fisher Information Metric). Given a parametric family p_\theta(x), the Fisher Information Matrix is

g_{ij}(\theta) = \mathbb{E}_{x \sim p_\theta}\!\left[\partial_i \log p_\theta(x) \;\partial_j \log p_\theta(x)\right].
\]  

Under regularity conditions, g_{ij}(\theta) = -\mathbb{E}[\partial_i\partial_j \log p_\theta(x)]. This matrix defines a Riemannian metric on the parameter manifold \Theta.

Definition 2.4 (Dependent Type Theory). We work in the Calculus of Inductive Constructions (CIC) with an infinite cumulative hierarchy of predicative universes \mathcal{U}_i and an impredicative universe of propositions \mathsf{Prop}. A term t : T is a proof that the type T is inhabited. An environment \Gamma assigns types to variables; the judgment \Gamma \vdash t : T denotes derivability. Judgmental equality t \equiv u : T is given by \beta,\delta,\iota,\zeta-reduction. The Lean 4 kernel is a verified implementation of CIC; if it accepts t : T, then T is inhabited in the set‑theoretic model, and acceptance of \bot : \mathsf{False} would imply inconsistency.

Definition 2.5 (Lyapunov Stability). For a continuous‑time dynamical system \dot{\theta} = f(\theta) with equilibrium \theta^*, a Lyapunov function is a continuously differentiable V(\theta) satisfying V(\theta^*) = 0, V(\theta) > 0 for \theta \neq \theta^*, and \dot{V}(\theta) = \nabla V \cdot f(\theta) \le 0. Asymptotic stability requires \dot{V} < 0 for \theta \neq \theta^*. In the discrete‑time stochastic case, a function V is a stochastic Lyapunov function if there exists c>0 such that \mathbb{E}[V(\theta_{k+1})\mid \mathcal{F}_k] - V(\theta_k) \le -c\, \alpha_k \|\nabla V(\theta_k)\|^2 for a step‑size sequence \alpha_k.

Definition 2.6 (Kolmogorov Complexity). For a fixed universal Turing machine U, the Kolmogorov complexity K_U(x) of a string x is the length of the shortest program p such that U(p) = x. Invariance up to an additive constant allows us to omit the subscript.

2.2 Information Geometry of Statistical Models

Let \mathcal{P} = \{p_\theta : \theta \in \Theta \subset \mathbb{R}^d\} be a smooth statistical manifold where \Theta is open. The Fisher metric g endows (\Theta, g) with a Riemannian structure. A fundamental result is the Cramér–Rao bound:

Lemma 2.1 (Cramér–Rao Bound). For any unbiased estimator \hat{\theta} of \theta,

\operatorname{Cov}_\theta[\hat{\theta}] \succeq g^{-1}(\theta).
\]  

This inequality sharpens the connection between the geometry of \Theta and the efficiency of learning: natural gradient descent on the KL loss moves along steepest directions with respect to g.

2.3 Calculus of Inductive Constructions and Formal Verification

CIC, as implemented in Lean 4, serves as the external oracle. Its kernel is a small, trusted piece of code that type‑checks terms. We state the soundness property:

Lemma 2.2 (Kernel Soundness). If \vdash t : T is accepted by the Lean 4 kernel, then T is inhabited in the set‑theoretic model of CIC. The kernel is complete with respect to the formal rules of CIC.

A central object for the hybrid loss is the type‑error distance. For a failed term t that was expected to have type T, define the set of valid inhabitants \mathcal{V}_T = \{ s \mid \vdash s : T \} (which may be empty if T is uninhabited). Let \phi : \text{Term} \to \mathbb{R}^{2048} be the embedding provided by the Transformer’s last hidden layer.

Definition 2.7 (Type‑Error Distance).

d_{\text{type}}(t, T) = 
\begin{cases}
\displaystyle \min_{s \in \mathcal{V}_T} \|\phi(t) - \phi(s)\|_2^2, & \mathcal{V}_T \neq \emptyset,\\[8pt]
\|\phi(t) - \phi_{\text{empty}}\|_2^2, & \mathcal{V}_T = \emptyset,
\end{cases}
\]  

where \phi_{\text{empty}} is a fixed penalty embedding chosen so that the gradient points away from the empty‑type region. We assume \phi is Lipschitz continuous (bounded weights and layer‑normalization guarantee this).

2.4 Stochastic Approximation and Robbins–Monro

The training process relies on stochastic gradient descent (SGD). We use the Robbins–Monro framework:

Theorem 2.3 (Robbins–Monro). Consider \theta_{k+1} = \theta_k - \alpha_k (\nabla V(\theta_k) + \xi_k), where \mathbb{E}[\xi_k \mid \mathcal{F}_k] = 0, \sup_k \mathbb{E}[\|\nabla V\|^2] < \infty, \sum \alpha_k = \infty, \sum \alpha_k^2 < \infty. Then \theta_k \to \{\theta : \nabla V(\theta) = 0\} almost surely.

2.5 The Stochastic Ceiling Theorem

We now formalize the collapse of self‑training language models.

Setup. A human‑authored dataset D_h = \{(x_i, y_i)\}_{i=1}^N is drawn from a distribution \mathcal{D}_h with density p_h(x,y). We train a model p_\theta(y|x) by minimizing the empirical cross‑entropy \mathbb{E}_{D_h}[-\log p_\theta(y|x)]; asymptotically p_\theta \to p_h if the model class is well‑specified.

Consider a self‑training loop: at step t, generate a synthetic dataset

D_s^{(t)} = \{(x_i, \hat{y}_i) \mid \hat{y}_i \sim p_{\theta_t}(\cdot|x_i),\; x_i \sim p_h(x)\},
\]  

and then update \theta_{t+1} = \arg\min_\theta \mathbb{E}_{D_s^{(t)}}[-\log p_\theta(y|x)].

Theorem 2.4 (Stochastic Ceiling). If the model is recursively retrained solely on its own outputs, then in the limit t \to \infty the predictive entropy vanishes almost surely for almost every prompt x, and the model’s distribution collapses to a point mass on the mode of its initial inductive biases. No new information is generated; the self‑training operator is a contraction on the space of non‑degenerate probability measures under the total‑variation metric.

Proof.
Regard the self‑training step as applying the operator T on the space of conditional distributions: (Tp)(y|x) = \mathbb{E}_{\hat{y} \sim p(\cdot|x)}[\delta_{\hat{y}}], i.e., the empirical distribution of the samples becomes the target for the next retraining. If the model class is sufficiently expressive, retraining performs an M‑projection, driving p_{\theta_{t+1}} toward Tp_{\theta_t}. In the idealised limit of infinite data and perfect optimisation, p_{\theta_{t+1}} = Tp_{\theta_t}.

Now consider the conditional entropy H(p_\theta(\cdot|x)) = \mathbb{E}_{\hat{y}\sim p_\theta}[-\log p_\theta(\hat{y}|x)]. Because p_{\theta_{t+1}} is fitted to samples from p_{\theta_t}, the process is monotone: H_{t+1} \le H_t. More sharply, view the map A: f \mapsto \mathbb{E}_{\hat{y}\sim f}[\delta_{\hat{y}}] on the simplex of probabilities over the output vocabulary. For any distribution f with support size at least 2, the operator is a strict contraction in total‑variation:

d_{TV}(A(f), A(g)) \le \frac{1}{2} d_{TV}(f,g),
\]  

when f,g possess unique modes. The only fixed points of A are point masses. By the Banach fixed‑point theorem, every initial distribution converges to a Dirac delta at \arg\max_y p_{\theta_0}(y|x) for each x. Hence H(p_{\theta_t}(\cdot|x)) \to 0 almost surely, and the model’s output becomes deterministic.

The only escape is to inject samples from an external distribution p^* with positive entropy that does not depend on \theta_t. In the SRNE, the Lean 4 kernel supplies a binary (hence non‑probabilistic) verification signal that breaks the contraction, as formalised in the next chapters. ∎

Corollary 2.5 (Autonomy Requirement). Unbounded self‑improvement requires a non‑probabilistic grounding oracle; otherwise the model degenerates to a trivial copy of its own initial mode.

