Chapter 3

Neuro‑Symbolic Architecture: The Type‑Driven Attention Mechanism

3.1 Architecture Overview

The SRNE combines a standard Transformer T_\theta with the Lean 4 kernel \mathcal{K}. Given an input prompt (e.g., a mathematical statement), the Transformer simultaneously predicts the target type T and a candidate proof term t. The term is then submitted to \mathcal{K} for verification. The kernel returns \top if \Gamma \vdash t : T holds, and otherwise returns \bot together with an error trace. This Boolean signal is the external ground truth that defeats the stochastic ceiling.

Specifications.

· Input Encoder: A 24‑layer Transformer with hidden size 2048, 32 attention heads, and relative positional encodings.
· Type Parsing Head: A linear layer with softmax over a vocabulary of type symbols (approximately 30 k tokens), trained to output a well‑formed type expression T from the prompt. A grammar constraint ensures well‑formedness.
· Term Generator (Decoder): A 12‑layer decoder stack with hidden size 2048, feed‑forward dimension 8192, rotary embeddings, and dropout 0.1. It cross‑attends to the encoder output and generates a sequence of tokens representing a formal term t.
· Kernel Interface: The generated term string is parsed by the Lean 4 frontend and submitted to the kernel. The kernel returns a Boolean verdict and, in case of failure, a minimal error message indicating the mismatched type or missing hypothesis.

3.2 Loss Function

The model is trained with a hybrid loss:

\mathcal{L}(\theta) = \mathcal{L}_{\text{NLL}}(\theta) + \lambda \mathcal{L}_{\text{type}}(\theta),

where \mathcal{L}_{\text{NLL}} is the standard next‑token cross‑entropy on human‑authored data, and

\mathcal{L}_{\text{type}}(\theta) = 
\begin{cases}
0, & \text{if } \Gamma \vdash t_\theta(x) : T(x), \\
d_{\text{type}}(t_\theta(x), T(x)) + \text{penalty}, & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}

The penalty term is the negative log‑likelihood of the error token sequence, discouraging known failure modes. The distance d_{\text{type}} is the squared Euclidean distance between the latent embeddings of the failed term and the nearest valid term (or the empty‑type penalty embedding if no inhabitant exists), as defined in Section 2.3.

3.3 Asymptotic Soundness

We now prove that any critical point of the combined loss that achieves arbitrarily small NLL loss and exactly zero type‑loss produces only well‑typed terms.

Theorem 3.1 (Asymptotic Soundness). Assume:

1. The kernel \mathcal{K} is sound and complete for CIC.
2. The embedding \phi: \text{Term} \to \mathbb{R}^{2048} is Lipschitz with constant L_\phi.
3. For any \theta with \mathbb{P}[\vdash t_\theta:T] < 1, the expected subgradient of \mathcal{L}_{\text{type}} satisfies \|\mathbb{E}[\nabla_\theta \mathcal{L}_{\text{type}}]\| \ge \delta > 0; i.e., on average the gradient points strictly toward the valid‑term region.
4. The Robbins–Monro conditions hold for the learning rate schedule.

Let V(\theta) = \mathcal{L}_{\text{NLL}}(\theta) + \lambda \mathcal{L}_{\text{type}}(\theta). Then every limit point \theta^* of the SGD trajectory satisfies \mathcal{L}_{\text{type}}(\theta^*) = 0 and \mathcal{L}_{\text{NLL}}(\theta^*) \le \epsilon. Hence the model almost surely generates only well‑typed terms.

Proof.
Define the set of sound parameters \Theta_{\text{sound}} = \{\theta \mid \mathbb{P}_{x}[\vdash t_\theta(x):T(x)] = 1\}. For \theta \notin \Theta_{\text{sound}}, we have \mathcal{L}_{\text{type}}(\theta) > 0. Under assumption 3, there exists a constant \delta > 0 such that the expected negative gradient of the type loss has norm at least \delta whenever the type‑error is non‑zero.

Consider the SGD update

\theta_{k+1} = \theta_k - \alpha_k (\nabla \mathcal{L}_{\text{NLL}}(\theta_k) + \lambda g_k^{\text{type}} + \xi_k),
\]  

where g_k^{\text{type}} is a subgradient of \mathcal{L}_{\text{type}} and \xi_k is martingale noise. Write \nabla V(\theta_k) for the true gradient of V. Because both \mathcal{L}_{\text{NLL}} and \mathcal{L}_{\text{type}} are Lipschitz, the noise has bounded variance, so by Theorem 2.3, \theta_k converges to the set of points where \nabla V(\theta) = 0 almost surely.

Now consider any point \theta with \mathcal{L}_{\text{type}}(\theta) > 0. At such a point, \|\mathbb{E}[\nabla \mathcal{L}_{\text{type}}]\| \ge \delta, so \|\nabla V(\theta)\| \ge \lambda\delta > 0. Hence \theta cannot be a stationary point of V. Therefore all limit points must satisfy \mathcal{L}_{\text{type}}(\theta^*) = 0, i.e. \theta^* \in \Theta_{\text{sound}}.

If additionally the model achieves \mathcal{L}_{\text{NLL}}(\theta^*) \le \epsilon, it continues to fit the human‑authored data while producing only kernel‑verifiable proofs. ∎

Remark 3.1. The coercivity assumption 3 can be replaced by a more refined constant: there exist c>0, p_0\in(0,1) such that for any \theta with \mathbb{P}[\text{valid}] \le 1-p_0, the expected type‑error gradient has magnitude at least c. This holds in the HoTT experiments: even infrequent errors produce large corrective gradients due to the distance penalty.

3.4 Discussion

The hybrid loss integrates the non‑probabilistic kernel signal into gradient‑based optimisation. The type‑error term acts as an attractor: it shapes the parameter landscape so that valid‑proof regions become basins of attraction. The result is a model that not only mimics human language but respects the hard constraints of formal logic.

