Chapter 10

Ordinal Analysis of Recursive Self‑Improvement

We now determine the precise proof‑theoretic strength of the SRNE at the fixed point \theta^*. This ordinal bound confirms that the system cannot prove its own consistency and quantifies the exact boundary of its deductive power.

10.1 Proof‑Theoretic Ordinals

For a theory \mathcal{T} containing arithmetic, the proof‑theoretic ordinal |\mathcal{T}| is the supremum of the order types of primitive recursive well‑orderings that \mathcal{T} can prove to be well‑founded. This ordinal characterises the Π⁰₂ consequences of \mathcal{T}.

The Calculus of Inductive Constructions with n universe levels and an impredicative Prop is a powerful theory. Its exact ordinal is a large recursive ordinal; it corresponds to the strength of Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory with n inaccessible cardinals. For the purposes of this dissertation, we denote this ordinal simply by |\mathcal{F}_0|; its precise notation (which lies far beyond the Veblen hierarchy) is not needed.

10.2 Ordinal of the SRNE Fixed Point

Theorem 10.1 (Ordinal Bound).
The SRNE fixed point \theta^* proves exactly the Π⁰₂ fragment of \mathcal{F}_0. Consequently, the proof‑theoretic ordinal of the set of theorems provable by the SRNE is |\mathcal{F}_0|.

Proof.
Upper bound. Every theorem proved by the SRNE is verified by the kernel, hence already a theorem of \mathcal{F}_0. Thus |\text{Th}(\theta^*)| \le |\mathcal{F}_0|.

Lower bound. Let \forall x \exists y R(x,y) be a Π⁰₂ sentence provable in \mathcal{F}_0, with R primitive recursive. Its proof yields a computable function f such that R(x,f(x)) holds for all x. The prover–verifier loop, in the limit of an exhaustive search, will eventually find and verify a proof term for this sentence. Because the sentence is Π⁰₂, its proof does not require transfinite induction beyond the strength of \mathcal{F}_0 itself; once found, it is permanently retained. Hence every Π⁰₂ theorem of \mathcal{F}_0 is eventually proved. Since the proof‑theoretic ordinal is determined by the provable well‑foundness of recursive ordinals (a Π⁰₂ property), the lower bound follows: |\text{Th}(\theta^*)| \ge |\mathcal{F}_0|. ∎

Corollary 10.2 (Gödelian Constraint).
\text{Con}(\mathcal{F}_0) is not in the Π⁰₂ fragment of \mathcal{F}_0; it is a Π⁰₁ statement. The SRNE cannot prove its own consistency. This places an absolute logical limit on recursive self‑improvement: the system is complete for \mathcal{F}_0 but cannot step outside it.

10.3 Reflection and Transfinite Extensions

A natural question is whether the SRNE can autonomously add stronger axioms. If the system were equipped with a meta‑kernel capable of verifying relative consistency proofs of the form \text{Con}(\mathcal{F}) \to \text{Con}(\mathcal{F} \cup \{\alpha\}), then it could, in principle, adopt new axioms along a well‑founded reflection path. However, this would require extending the Ethics Layer to demand that any such addition preserves consistency, and the meta‑orchestrator would need to check the reflection step. This possibility is left as an open problem (Chapter 16) because it lies outside the core SRNE architecture.

