Chapter 11

Deployment Architecture and Graceful Degradation

While previous chapters establish theoretical guarantees, the SRNE must function as a reliable service even when its kernel is temporarily unavailable. This chapter specifies the Layered Logical Service Mesh (LLSM) and proves that a hardware‑enforced interlock prevents the system from silently falling back to a purely stochastic (and potentially unsound) mode.

11.1 System Topology

The LLSM consists of three tiers, as defined in Chapter 8:

· Tier 1 – Edge Inference Nodes: Lightweight Transformers that serve user requests with a strict latency budget. They produce proof obligations.
· Tier 2 – Verification Cluster: Stateless Lean 4 kernel instances that type‑check obligations and return verdicts.
· Tier 0 – Meta‑Orchestrator: Runs the self‑improvement loop and manages the prover–verifier self‑play.

11.2 The Hardware Watchdog and Token‑Bucket Safety

A critical risk is that a kernel outage could force all queries into “probabilistic mode,” bypassing type‑checking altogether. To prevent this, each edge node contains an FPGA‑based watchdog that implements a token‑bucket mechanism.

Bucket rules:

· Each verified response adds 1 token to the bucket.
· Each unverified response consumes \frac{1-\epsilon}{\epsilon} tokens, where \epsilon is the maximum tolerated unverified fraction.

The bucket has a finite capacity B. If the bucket is empty, the edge node must refuse generation (or wait for kernel recovery) rather than emit an unverified response. The token consumption rate is chosen so that, in steady state with a fraction \epsilon of unverified traffic, the bucket remains balanced.

11.3 Graceful Degradation Theorem

Theorem 11.1 (Graceful Degradation).
Let \epsilon > 0 be the allowed unverified fraction. Choose the bucket capacity

B = \frac{1-\epsilon}{\epsilon} \cdot U_{\max} \cdot \text{MTTR} \cdot \log\left(\frac{1}{\delta}\right),
\]  

where U_{\max} is the maximum rate of unverified responses, and \text{MTTR} is the mean time to kernel repair. Then, for any observation window W, the probability that the unverified fraction exceeds \epsilon is at most \delta. Thus, with probability 1-\delta, the system never silently degrades to a purely stochastic generator.

Proof sketch. During normal operation, verified traffic supplies tokens at a rate that matches or exceeds the allowed unverified drain. When the kernel fails, only the drain remains. The bucket empties only if the outage lasts long enough to exhaust B tokens. Using a queueing model and Hoeffding’s inequality for the cumulative unverified count during an outage, the probability of emptying within the expected MTTR is bounded by \delta. A union bound over the number of outages in W yields the result. ∎

Corollary 11.2. The hardware watchdog is a non‑bypassable safety interlock. Even a full software failure in the edge node cannot cause the watchdog to permit an unverified response when the bucket is empty. The root‑of‑trust lies in the FPGA, which is physically independent.

11.4 Speculative Verification

To meet latency targets, the edge node may send several candidate proof sketches in parallel to the verification cluster. The first valid verdict terminates the query. Redundant failures cost no additional tokens, and only the successful verification adds a token. This speculative strategy keeps the expected latency low without compromising the token‑bucket invariant.

