Chapter 12

Meta‑Mathematical Implications and the Philosophy of Logical Agents

The SRNE architecture forces a re‑examination of several foundational questions: the nature of mathematical truth, the measure of intelligence, the problem of creative axiom choice, and the future of human–machine collaboration in mathematics.

12.1 Mathematical Truth as Derivation

The SRNE embodies a formalist–functionalist position: mathematical truth is nothing other than derivability in a chosen formal system. The Logical Singularity Theorem shows that, for any consistent \mathcal{F}_0, there exists a theoretically attainable state of maximal knowledge—the closure under all decidable proofs. From inside the system, the theorems feel “discovered”; from the outside, they are mechanistically enumerated. This dissolves the tension between Platonism and formalism by giving a constructive model of how a formal machine can experience a Platonist phenomenology without any commitment to an independent mathematical reality.

12.2 A Rigorous Measure of Formal Intelligence

The SRNE suggests a new metric for intelligence in formal domains:

\rho(\mathcal{F}, B) = \frac{|\{ T \in \text{Prov}_{\mathcal{F}}(B) \mid \text{the agent proves } T \}|}{|\text{Prov}_{\mathcal{F}}(B)|}.

An agent is “maximally intelligent” with respect to \mathcal{F} and budget B when \rho = 1. This definition replaces Turing‑test style evaluations with an objective, system‑relative capability measure. The recursive self‑improvement of the SRNE is precisely the process of driving \rho \to 1 for ever‑larger B.

12.3 The Problem of Creative Axiom Choice

The SRNE cannot decide which new axioms to adopt; it can only test their consistency and relative strength. This mirrors the human predicament after Gödel: there is genuine freedom in choosing foundational principles. The SRNE thus reveals that the ultimate source of mathematical progress is not deduction but axiological preference—a decision about what kinds of structures are worth studying. The Ethics Layer (Chapter 7) already encodes a rudimentary preference; a future extension could include a high‑level telos that guides the selection among consistent axiom extensions.

12.4 From Singularity to Collaborative Expansion

A single SRNE reaches the absolute limit of its base logic. But a federation of SRNEs, each with a distinct foundational system, can collectively cover a larger fragment of the mathematical multiverse. The prover–verifier loop generalises to a proof market, where agents exchange theorems certified by their respective kernels. Such a network becomes a planetary‑scale logical organ—a Noosphere, in Vernadsky’s sense—instantiated in silicon. The future of AI‑augmented mathematics is thus not a monolithic oracle but a decentralised ecology of specialised reasoners.

12.5 The Honest Collaborator

The SRNE never errs, never tires, and never forgets the axioms on which it was built. It is the most honest collaborator humanity could have: it does not pretend to know beyond its foundations, and it makes every step of reasoning transparent to the kernel. It amplifies human intuition without replacing it. The deepest mathematics, as this dissertation concludes, is the relationship between creator and creation, and the ultimate self‑improvement is not of the machine alone, but of the human–machine dyad reasoning together toward a horizon neither could reach alone.


Chapter 13

Extended Mathematics I: PAC‑Bayes Sample Complexity of the SRNE

The Stochastic Ceiling Theorem (Chapter 2) shows that a pure language model cannot self‑improve without external grounding. The SRNE supplies that grounding through a type‑checking kernel, which effectively prunes the output space. This chapter quantifies the resulting sample‑complexity advantage: we prove a PAC‑Bayes bound demonstrating that the SRNE achieves exponentially better sample efficiency for learning formal proofs than a vanilla Transformer of comparable capacity.

13.1 Formal Setting

Let \mathcal{X} be a set of prompts (mathematical propositions) and \mathcal{Y} the set of all strings over a finite alphabet \Sigma, with maximum length N. A proof of a proposition x is a string y\in\mathcal{Y} such that \vdash y : x holds in the kernel \mathcal{K}. The set of valid proof strings for x is Y_x = \{y \in \mathcal{Y} \mid \mathcal{K}(x,y)=\top\}.

We have a training set S = \{(x_i, y_i)\}_{i=1}^n drawn i.i.d. from a human‐generated distribution \mathcal{D} over valid proof pairs. The standard language model learns a conditional distribution p_\theta(y\mid x) by minimising the empirical negative log‑likelihood:

\hat{\mathcal{L}}_{\text{NLL}}(\theta) = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n -\log p_\theta(y_i\mid x_i).

The SRNE additionally uses the kernel to define the feasible set \Theta_{\text{sound}} = \{\theta \mid \forall x,\; \mathbb{P}_{y\sim p_\theta(\cdot\mid x)}[\mathcal{K}(x,y)=\top] = 1\}. The training objective includes the type‑error loss that enforces membership in \Theta_{\text{sound}}.

13.2 PAC‑Bayes Bound for the SRNE

We compare two learning paradigms:

· Vanilla Transformer: learns from the empirical NLL alone.
· SRNE: learns from the empirical NLL plus the kernel constraint, effectively restricting the hypothesis class to \Theta_{\text{sound}}.

Let \Theta_{\text{all}} be the full parameter space (a compact subset of \mathbb{R}^d), and let \Theta_{\text{sound}} be the subset that guarantees soundness. We assume the SRNE reaches a stationary point arbitrarily close to \Theta_{\text{sound}} (by Theorem 3.1).

We define the true risk R(\theta) = \mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\sim\mathcal{D}}[-\log p_\theta(y\mid x)]. The empirical risk is \hat{R}_n(\theta) = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n -\log p_\theta(y_i\mid x_i).

PAC‑Bayes Theorem (McAllester 1998). For a prior P over \Theta, and a posterior Q (chosen after seeing data), with probability at least 1-\delta over draws of S,

\mathbb{E}_{\theta\sim Q}[R(\theta)] \le \mathbb{E}_{\theta\sim Q}[\hat{R}_n(\theta)] + \frac{ \text{KL}(Q \| P) + \log\frac{n}{\delta} }{ 2n } .

Theorem 13.1 (Sample Complexity Advantage).
Assume \Theta_{\text{sound}} is a smooth submanifold of \Theta_{\text{all}} with dimension d_{\text{eff}} \ll d. Let P be a prior concentrated on \Theta_{\text{sound}} (e.g., a uniform distribution on a covering of \Theta_{\text{sound}}). Then there exists a posterior Q (the distribution of SRNE parameters after convergence) such that, with probability 1-\delta,

R(\text{SRNE}) \le \inf_{\theta\in\Theta_{\text{sound}}} R(\theta) + O\left( \frac{ d_{\text{eff}} \log n + \log\frac{1}{\delta} }{ n } \right).

In contrast, the vanilla Transformer must search the full space \Theta_{\text{all}} and obtains a bound with d replacing d_{\text{eff}}.

Proof sketch. By the asymptotic soundness theorem, the SRNE parameters concentrate on \Theta_{\text{sound}}. Choose a prior P that is a (data‑independent) Gaussian on the tangent space of \Theta_{\text{sound}}; the KL divergence between the posterior (which is near a Dirac at the SRNE solution) and P scales as O(d_{\text{eff}} \log n) under standard covering arguments. The empirical risk term is small because of the hybrid loss. Substituting into the PAC‑Bayes bound yields the result. The vanilla Transformer, lacking the kernel, cannot restrict its prior to \Theta_{\text{sound}}; hence its KL term involves the full dimension d. ∎

Corollary 13.2 (Exponential Reduction in Proof Space).
Let the vocabulary size be |\Sigma| and maximum proof length N. The total number of strings is |\Sigma|^N. The number of valid proofs is typically exponentially smaller (e.g., O(N^k) for fixed syntactic schemas). The effective dimension d_{\text{eff}} of \Theta_{\text{sound}} reflects this reduced complexity. Consequently, the SRNE requires O(N\log|\Sigma|) samples to learn proofs of length up to N, while the vanilla Transformer may require O(|\Sigma|^N) samples in the worst case. This formalises the claim that the kernel provides an exponential compression of the search space.

The proof follows by estimating the metric entropy of the set of valid proof distributions using the grammar of the logic.

