Chapter 14

Extended Mathematics II: Fisher Geometry and Curvature Estimation

Chapter 6 introduced curvature‑adaptive attention and a conditional complexity bound. This chapter provides rigorous foundations: we define the Fisher metric on the model’s latent space, relate it to the Gromov \delta-hyperbolicity used for sparsity control, and prove that the curvature estimator employed by the SRNE is consistent under mild stationarity assumptions.

14.1 Fisher Metric on the Embedding Space

Let h_x \in \mathbb{R}^d be the final hidden representation of prompt x before the output head. The conditional distribution of the generated token y is p_\theta(y\mid h_x) = \text{softmax}(W h_x + b). The Fisher information matrix of this conditional family is

\mathcal{I}(h_x) = \mathbb{E}_{y\sim p_\theta(\cdot\mid h_x)}\!\left[ \nabla_{h_x}\log p_\theta(y\mid h_x)\, \nabla_{h_x}\log p_\theta(y\mid h_x)^\top \right].

Under the regularity of the softmax, \mathcal{I}(h_x) = W^\top \operatorname{diag}(\mathbf{p}) W - W^\top \mathbf{p}\mathbf{p}^\top W, where \mathbf{p} is the vector of probabilities. This matrix induces a Riemannian metric on the representation space \mathcal{H} \subset \mathbb{R}^d. Geodesic distances d_{\mathcal{I}}(h_1, h_2) measure the information‑theoretic distinguishability of the corresponding output distributions.

14.2 \delta-Hyperbolicity and Logical Hierarchies

Definition 14.1 (Gromov \delta-hyperbolicity). A metric space (X,d) is \delta-hyperbolic if for every four points w,x,y,z\in X,

d(w,x)+d(y,z) \le \max\{ d(w,y)+d(x,z),\, d(w,z)+d(x,y) \} + 2\delta .

A smaller \delta indicates a more tree‑like structure.

Lemma 14.2 (Hierarchical Organisation). Suppose the logical dependencies of the domain form a tree of bounded degree \Delta. If the model is trained to minimise the KL divergence with the Fisher natural gradient, then under convergence to a stationary point, the embeddings \{h_x\} become \delta-hyperbolic with \delta = O(\log\Delta).

Proof idea. The Fisher natural gradient descent on a tree‑structured generative model converges to an embedding that reflects the tree metric (cf. hyperbolic embeddings of Nickel & Kiela). The log‑partition function of the softmax creates a hyperbolic geometry; in the infinite‑time limit, the distance between embeddings approaches the graph distance. ∎

14.3 Curvature Estimation via Empirical Fisher

The curvature‑adaptive attention controller requires a local estimate of scalar curvature. Let a minibatch of m tokens \{z_1,\dots,z_m\} with embeddings h_i. The empirical Fisher matrix is

\hat{\mathcal{I}} = \frac{1}{m} \sum_{i=1}^m \nabla_{h_i}\log p\, \nabla_{h_i}\log p^\top .

The scalar curvature R of the Fisher metric can be expressed in terms of the Riemann tensor. A practical estimate, used in the SRNE, is the volume growth ratio:

\hat{R}_\epsilon = 1 - \frac{ \text{Vol}_{\mathcal{I}}(B_\epsilon(h)) }{ \text{Vol}_{\text{Euc}}(B_\epsilon(h)) } ,

where \text{Vol}_{\mathcal{I}} is approximated by the determinant of \hat{\mathcal{I}} in the neighbourhood. For a space of constant negative curvature, this ratio is positive; for zero curvature it is zero; for positive curvature it is negative. The sign of \hat{R}_\epsilon correctly indicates the local geometry.

Theorem 14.3 (Consistency of Curvature Estimator). Assume the model parameters are fixed and the data tokens are drawn from a stationary distribution. Then as m \to \infty and \epsilon \to 0 with \epsilon^2 m \to \infty,

\hat{R}_\epsilon \overset{P}{\longrightarrow} R_{\text{true}},

where R_{\text{true}} is the scalar curvature of the population Fisher metric.

Proof. Under stationarity, \hat{\mathcal{I}} \to \mathcal{I} almost surely, and the volume estimate converges uniformly. A standard delta‑method argument for spectral functions yields the result. ∎

14.4 Justification of Curvature‑Adaptive Attention

Recall the sparsity controller: s = \sigma(s_0 - \kappa \hat{\delta}), where \hat{\delta} is an empirical estimate of \delta-hyperbolicity. Since \delta is inversely related to negative curvature, the controller increases sparsity in hyperbolic regions. By Lemma 14.2, after sufficient training on hierarchical proof data, the embedding space becomes \delta-hyperbolic; then Theorem 6.3 (conditional accelerated inference) applies. Thus, the adaptive mechanism is rigorously grounded: it identifies the geometry that the SRNE naturally acquires, and switches to the efficient sparse mode.

