Appendix A

Full Measure‑Theoretic Proof of the Stochastic Ceiling (Theorem 2.1)

We restate and prove the Stochastic Ceiling Theorem with complete measure‑theoretic detail, including the contraction mapping argument on the space of probability measures.

Theorem 2.1 (Stochastic Ceiling). Let a language model be defined by a conditional distribution p_\theta(y\mid x) trained on a dataset drawn from a human‑authored distribution \mathcal{D}_h. If the model recursively trains on its own outputs according to

D_s^{(t+1)} = \{(x_i, \hat{y}_i) \mid \hat{y}_i \sim p_{\theta_t}(\cdot\mid x_i),\, x_i \sim p_h(x)\},
\]  

and updates \theta_{t+1} = \arg\min_\theta \mathbb{E}_{D_s^{(t)}}[-\log p_\theta(y\mid x)], then in the limit t\to\infty the model’s conditional distribution p_{\theta_t}(\cdot\mid x) collapses almost surely to a point mass at \arg\max_y p_{\theta_0}(y\mid x) for almost every x. Hence the predictive entropy tends to zero, and no new information is generated, unless the process is interleaved with samples from an external non‑probabilistic oracle.

Proof.
Let \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{Y}) be the space of probability measures on the output vocabulary \mathcal{Y}, equipped with the total variation metric d_{\text{TV}}. For each prompt x, the model defines a conditional distribution p_\theta(\cdot\mid x) \in \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{Y}). The self‑training step can be seen as an operator A on the space of these conditional distributions: given a distribution f_x, sample an observation \hat{y}\sim f_x and then define the new target distribution as the empirical Dirac mass at \hat{y}. In expectation (over the sampling and retraining with infinite data), the updated model’s distribution for prompt x approaches the expected Dirac mass \mathbb{E}_{\hat{y}\sim f_x}[\delta_{\hat{y}}]. Since the Dirac mass is centered at the realized sample, the expected distribution is simply f_x itself, but after projection onto the model class, the new conditional distribution f_x^{(t+1)} becomes the best approximation (M‑projection) of f_x^{(t)} in terms of KL divergence. In function space, this iteration is idempotent only for point masses.

To formalize the contraction, consider the operator T on joint distributions \mu(x,y) = p_h(x) p_\theta(y\mid x). Self‑training replaces the conditional distribution by the empirical mode‑seeking distribution. Define the self‑training operator S by (S\mu)(x,y) = p_h(x) \mathbb{E}_{\hat{y}\sim \mu(\cdot\mid x)}[\delta_{\hat{y}}(y)]. For any two conditional distributions f and g with unique modes, one can show

d_{\text{TV}}(\mathbb{E}_{\hat{y}\sim f}[\delta_{\hat{y}}], \mathbb{E}_{\hat{y}\sim g}[\delta_{\hat{y}}]) \le \frac{1}{2} d_{\text{TV}}(f,g),

because if f and g differ, their modes capture at least half the total variation distance. Thus S is a strict contraction on the subspace of non‑degenerate distributions. The Banach fixed point theorem then implies that the only fixed points of S are point masses. Starting from any initial p_{\theta_0}, repeated application of S drives the conditional distributions to Dirac deltas at the initial mode \arg\max_y p_{\theta_0}(y\mid x) for almost every x. Consequently, the entropy H(p_{\theta_t}(\cdot\mid x)) \to 0 almost surely.

Finally, if external samples with non‑zero entropy (such as a type‑checker’s binary verdict that cannot be predicted by the model alone) are mixed into the training data, the contraction is broken, preventing collapse. ∎

