Appendix B

Lipschitz Continuity of the Term Embedding (Lemma 1.11)

Lemma 1.11. The embedding map \phi : \text{Term} \to \mathbb{R}^{2048} obtained from the Transformer’s last hidden layer is Lipschitz continuous, provided all weight matrices have bounded spectral norm and the layer normalization uses a fixed small epsilon.

Proof.
The Transformer stack consists of a series of multi‑head self‑attention layers and feed‑forward networks, each followed by residual connections and layer normalization. The input term is tokenized into a sequence of one‑hot vectors (or learned embeddings). Let the sequence length be at most L. For any two input sequences t, s of tokens, the Euclidean distance between their initial embeddings is bounded by 2\sqrt{L} (since embeddings lie on a bounded sphere). Each attention layer computes outputs of the form \text{softmax}(QK^\top/\sqrt{d_k}) V, where Q,K,V are linear projections of the input. The softmax is 1‑Lipschitz in the standard Euclidean metric, and the linear transformations have bounded operator norms by assumption. Composition preserves Lipschitz properties. The residual connections and layer normalization (with a small constant \epsilon in the denominator) are also Lipschitz. Therefore, the mapping from the discrete token sequence to the final hidden state \phi(t) is Lipschitz with constant L_\phi depending on the maximum spectral norm and the number of layers. ∎

