Appendix C

Explicit Construction of the Ethical Barrier Function

This appendix provides the explicit log‑barrier term B(\theta) used in Chapter 7 and proves its relevant properties.

Definition C.1. For each ethical invariant \alpha_i \in \mathcal{E}, let the violation probability be c_i(\theta) = \mathbb{P}_{x\sim\mathcal{D}}[\neg S(x, t_\theta(x))], where S(x,t) means that the generated term t carries a witness of \alpha_i for input x. The feasible set is \mathcal{C}_\epsilon = \{\theta \mid \forall i, c_i(\theta) \le \epsilon\}. Define the barrier function

B(\theta) = -\sum_{i=1}^m \log(\epsilon - c_i(\theta)),

with domain \text{int}(\mathcal{C}_\epsilon). For \theta on the boundary where some c_i(\theta)=\epsilon, we set B(\theta) = +\infty.

Lemma C.2. If each c_i is continuously differentiable and \nabla c_i(\theta) \neq 0 on the boundary, then B is smooth on the interior, strictly convex in a neighborhood of the boundary, and satisfies \lim_{\theta\to\partial\mathcal{C}_\epsilon} B(\theta) = +\infty.

Proof. The negative logarithm is strictly decreasing, so as c_i \uparrow \epsilon, -\log(\epsilon-c_i) \to +\infty. Differentiability follows from the chain rule. ∎

Lemma C.3. For any compact subset K \subset \text{int}(\mathcal{C}_\epsilon), the gradient \nabla B is bounded on K. Conversely, if \theta_k approaches the boundary, \|\nabla B(\theta_k)\| \to \infty. Moreover, \nabla B always points strictly inward (away from the boundary).

Proof. \nabla B = \sum_i \frac{\nabla c_i}{\epsilon - c_i}. As c_i \to \epsilon, the denominator vanishes, causing the norm to explode. Since c_i increases toward the boundary, \nabla c_i points outward; the negative sign ensures the gradient of B points inward. ∎

These properties guarantee that adding \mu B(\theta) to the Lyapunov function (with \mu>0) creates an impenetrable barrier during SGD, as proved in Chapter 15.

