Appendix D

Proof of Improvement Monotonicity (Lemma 5.2)

Lemma 5.2 (Improvement Monotonicity). Assume the SRNE updates parameters by minimizing a loss that includes the negative log‑likelihood of all previously successful proof traces. Then the set of propositions the prover can prove within M attempts is non‑shrinking across self‑improvement cycles.

Proof.
Let S_k be the set of valid proof traces produced in cycle k. The training loss for cycle k+1 is

\mathcal{L}^{(k+1)}(\theta) = \mathbb{E}_{D_h}[-\log p_\theta] + \lambda \mathcal{L}_{\text{type}} + \nu \sum_{(x,t)\in \bigcup_{j\le k} S_j} -\log p_\theta(t\mid x),

where \nu>0 is a weighting factor. Because the model starts from \theta_k and uses a trust‑region update, the parameter step is \Delta\theta \approx -\alpha \nabla \mathcal{L}^{(k+1)}(\theta_k). The gradient contains a strong attraction toward previously successful traces. Consequently, the model’s probability of generating those traces does not decrease substantially. More formally, for any (x,t)\in S_k, the log‑probability \log p_{\theta_{k+1}}(t\mid x) is at least \log p_{\theta_k}(t\mid x) - \alpha \beta, where \beta is a bound on the gradient change. By choosing \alpha small enough (as in the Lyapunov‑stable rule), the probability mass on each successful proof is preserved within a factor. Hence, the prover can reproduce previous proofs, and the provable set expands monotonically. ∎

