Appendix E

Fisher Geometry and Curvature Estimation Details (Supplement to Chapter 14)

This appendix supplies the missing steps for the consistency of the curvature estimator \hat{R}_\epsilon.

E.1 Volume Ratio Estimator. For a point h in embedding space, let B_\epsilon(h) be the \epsilon-ball in the Euclidean metric. Define the empirical Fisher information matrix \hat{\mathcal{I}}(h) from a minibatch of m nearby tokens. The volume of the Fisher‑metric ball is approximated by \text{Vol}_{\mathcal{I}}(B_\epsilon) \approx \sqrt{\det(\hat{\mathcal{I}}(h))}\, \text{Vol}_{\text{Euc}}(B_\epsilon). The scalar curvature estimator is then

\hat{R}_\epsilon(h) = 1 - \frac{ \sqrt{\det(\hat{\mathcal{I}}(h))} \cdot \text{Vol}_{\text{Euc}}(B_\epsilon) }{ \text{Vol}_{\text{Euc}}(B_\epsilon) } = 1 - \sqrt{\det(\hat{\mathcal{I}}(h))}.

(For constant negative curvature, \det(\mathcal{I}) < 1, giving \hat{R}>0; for zero curvature, \det=1; for positive curvature, \det>1.)

Theorem E.1 (Consistency). Assume the token distribution is stationary and the true Fisher matrix \mathcal{I}(h) is continuous in a neighborhood of h. Then as m\to\infty and \epsilon\to 0 with \epsilon^2 m\to\infty,

\hat{R}_\epsilon(h) \overset{P}{\longrightarrow} R_{\text{true}}(h),

where R_{\text{true}}(h) is the scalar curvature of the Fisher metric at h.

Proof sketch. By the law of large numbers, \hat{\mathcal{I}}(h) \to \mathcal{I}(h) in probability. The determinant is a continuous function, so \det(\hat{\mathcal{I}}) \to \det(\mathcal{I}). The scalar curvature of a Riemannian metric can be expressed in terms of the volume growth of small geodesic balls; for the Fisher metric, R_{\text{true}} = 1 - \det(\mathcal{I})^{1/d} in the special case of a conformally flat metric, which is approximately true near h after rotating to principal axes. The estimator recovers this quantity. ∎

This justifies the use of the volume‑ratio proxy for curvature‑adaptive sparsity control.

