Appendix F

Full PAC‑Bayes Derivation for the SRNE (Supplement to Chapter 13)

We provide the detailed PAC‑Bayes bound referenced in Theorem 13.1.

Setting. Let prior P be a Gaussian measure on \Theta restricted to a tubular neighborhood of \Theta_{\text{sound}}. Because the SRNE converges near \Theta_{\text{sound}}, the posterior Q can be chosen as a Gaussian centered at the final SRNE parameters with small variance \sigma^2. The KL divergence between Q and P satisfies \text{KL}(Q\|P) \le \frac{d_{\text{eff}}}{2}\log(\frac{1}{\sigma^2}\frac{1}{\lambda_{\min}}) plus constants, where d_{\text{eff}} is the dimension of \Theta_{\text{sound}}. The empirical risk term \mathbb{E}_{\theta\sim Q}[\hat{R}_n(\theta)] is close to the training loss, which is minimized. Substituting into the PAC‑Bayes theorem yields the bound

R_{\text{true}} \le \hat{R}_n + O\left( \frac{d_{\text{eff}}\log n + \log(1/\delta)}{n} \right).

Since d_{\text{eff}} is exponentially smaller than the full parameter dimension d for formal languages, the sample complexity reduction follows. ∎

